---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SdeSim from "../components/SdeSim.astro";
import SolvabilityMap from "../components/SolvabilityMap.astro";
import QuadraticVariation from "../components/QuadraticVariation.astro";
import Math from "../components/Math.astro";
import { competitions } from "../data/experience";

const smt = competitions.find((c) => c.name === "Stanford Math Tournament")!;
---

<Base
  title="Research, Jamal Mammadzada"
  description="Research by Jamal Mammadzada: an extended essay on the analytical solvability of one-dimensional stochastic differential equations, and a physics investigation into how temperature affects capacitor discharge."
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="mono">Research</p>
    <h1 class="h-lg">Research and writing</h1>
    <p class="lede narrow">
      Two pieces of formal research: an extended essay on how far Itô calculus
      actually gets you when solving stochastic differential equations, and a
      completed physics investigation into temperature and capacitor discharge
      that came out of robotics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="paper">
    <article class="paper">
      <header class="paper-head">
        <div>
          <p class="mono paper-kicker">
            IB Extended Essay · Mathematics · Started 2025 · 4,000 words
          </p>
          <h2 class="h-md" id="paper">
            Analytical solvability of one-dimensional SDEs
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="status is-done mono">Written, awaiting submission</p>
      </header>

      <div class="narrow paper-body">
        <p class="rq">
          To what extent does Itô calculus enable analytical solutions of
          one-dimensional stochastic differential equations arising from standard
          Brownian motion?
        </p>

        <p>
          The starting observation is that "solvable" isn't one thing. A model
          might give you an explicit formula for the path, or a known
          distribution at a fixed time, or closed expressions for moments, without
          giving you all three. Itô calculus applies to all four models
          in the paper. What varies is how much exactness you actually get out
          of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The setup is standard. A Wiener process{" "}
          <Math label="W of t">W<sub>t</sub></Math> is the formal model of
          Brownian motion: continuous everywhere, differentiable nowhere, which
          is why ordinary calculus can't handle it. A one-dimensional Itô
          process is written
        </p>

        <Math
          display
          n="1"
          label="d X t equals a of X t and t, d t, plus b of X t and t, d W t"
        >
          <span class="op">d</span>X<sub>t</sub> = a(X<sub>t</sub>, t)
          <span class="op">d</span>t + b(X<sub>t</sub>, t)
          <span class="op">d</span>W<sub>t</sub>
        </Math>

        <p>
          where <Math label="a">a</Math> is the drift and{" "}
          <Math label="b">b</Math> the diffusion. Itô's formula is the chain rule
          for such a process, and it carries a second-order term that ordinary
          calculus has no reason to keep. For a smooth{" "}
          <Math label="f of X t and t">f(X<sub>t</sub>, t)</Math>,
        </p>

        <Math
          display
          n="2"
          label="d f equals partial f partial t plus a times partial f partial x plus one half b squared times second partial f partial x squared, all d t, plus b times partial f partial x, d W t"
        >
          <span class="op">d</span>f = (
          <span class="frac"><span>∂f</span><span>∂t</span></span> + a
          <span class="frac"><span>∂f</span><span>∂x</span></span> +
          <span class="frac"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span>
          b<sup>2</sup>
          <span class="frac"><span>∂<sup>2</sup>f</span><span>∂x<sup>2</sup></span></span>
          ) <span class="op">d</span>t + b
          <span class="frac"><span>∂f</span><span>∂x</span></span>
          <span class="op">d</span>W<sub>t</sub>
        </Math>

        <p>
          That{" "}
          <Math label="one half b squared, second derivative term">
            ½ b<sup>2</sup> ∂<sup>2</sup>f/∂x<sup>2</sup>
          </Math>{" "}
          term is the whole difference. It exists because{" "}
          <Math label="d W squared equals d t">
            (<span class="op">d</span>W)<sup>2</sup> =
            <span class="op">d</span>t
          </Math>, which is not something a smooth function ever does.
        </p>

        <p>
          That second-order term is worth pausing on, because it's the whole
          reason stochastic calculus looks different from the calculus everyone
          learns first. Subdivide a Brownian path and add up the squared
          increments: the sum doesn't vanish the way it would for a smooth
          curve. It converges to the elapsed time. Try it below.
        </p>
      </div>

      <QuadraticVariation />

      <div class="narrow paper-body">
        <p>
          The comparison is the part I designed deliberately. Each of the four
          models changes exactly one structural feature, which turns the survey
          into something closer to a controlled experiment.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="model-table">
        <div class="model-row">
          <div class="model-name">
            <p class="mono model-tag">GBM</p>
            <p class="model-h">Geometric Brownian motion</p>
          </div>
          <Math label="d S t equals mu S t d t plus sigma S t d W t">
            <span class="op">d</span>S<sub>t</sub> = μS<sub>t</sub>
            <span class="op">d</span>t + σS<sub>t</sub>
            <span class="op">d</span>W<sub>t</sub>
          </Math>
          <p class="model-note">
            Multiplicative diffusion. Becomes additive under{" "}
            <Math label="log S t"><span class="op">log</span> S<sub>t</sub></Math>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="model-row">
          <div class="model-name">
            <p class="mono model-tag">OU</p>
            <p class="model-h">Ornstein–Uhlenbeck</p>
          </div>
          <Math label="d X t equals theta times mu minus X t, d t, plus sigma d W t">
            <span class="op">d</span>X<sub>t</sub> = θ(μ − X<sub>t</sub>)
            <span class="op">d</span>t + σ
            <span class="op">d</span>W<sub>t</sub>
          </Math>
          <p class="model-note">
            Linear drift, constant diffusion. Gaussian at every fixed time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="model-row">
          <div class="model-name">
            <p class="mono model-tag">CIR</p>
            <p class="model-h">Cox–Ingersoll–Ross</p>
          </div>
          <Math label="d X t equals theta times mu minus X t, d t, plus sigma root X t d W t">
            <span class="op">d</span>X<sub>t</sub> = θ(μ − X<sub>t</sub>)
            <span class="op">d</span>t + σ<span class="sqrt"><span>X<sub>t</sub></span></span>
            <span class="op">d</span>W<sub>t</sub>
          </Math>
          <p class="model-note">
            Square-root diffusion, with a boundary at{" "}
            <Math label="zero">0</Math>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="model-row">
          <div class="model-name">
            <p class="mono model-tag">SL</p>
            <p class="model-h">Stochastic logistic</p>
          </div>
          <Math label="d X t equals r X t times one minus X t over K, d t, plus sigma X t d W t">
            <span class="op">d</span>X<sub>t</sub> = rX<sub>t</sub>(1 −
            <span class="frac"><span>X<sub>t</sub></span><span>K</span></span>)
            <span class="op">d</span>t + σX<sub>t</sub>
            <span class="op">d</span>W<sub>t</sub>
          </Math>
          <p class="model-note">
            Nonlinear quadratic drift with multiplicative diffusion.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="narrow paper-body">

        <p>
          The result is a gradient rather than a line. GBM and OU satisfy all
          three criteria, giving explicit path formulas, recognizable finite-time
          distributions, and closed expressions for moments. CIR loses the
          elementary path formula but keeps an exact transition law, a noncentral
          chi-square, precisely because of its square-root diffusion and its
          behavior near zero. The logistic model has an explicit pathwise
          solution after a reciprocal transformation, yet its dependence on an
          exponential Brownian integral blocks a comparably simple finite-time
          distribution.
        </p>

        <p>
          So drift, diffusion, and boundary behavior between them determine which
          kind of exact result you can have, to a large but genuinely limited extent.
          That's the argument.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="figure-block">
        <p class="narrow figure-intro">
          The claim, laid out. Two things decide whether an exact solution
          exists: what the drift depends on, and what the diffusion depends on.
          Nonlinear drift turns out to be survivable. Nonlinear diffusion is
          where it stops.
        </p>

        <SolvabilityMap />
      </div>

      <div class="figure-block">
        <p class="narrow figure-intro">
          The same four processes, simulated. Each is integrated with the same
          scheme over the same interval, so what you see is the structure of the
          model rather than an artifact of the method.
        </p>

        <SdeSim />
      </div>

      <div class="narrow">
        <details class="tech">
          <summary>What Euler–Maruyama is doing, and why it's not free</summary>
          <p>
            It's the direct analogue of Euler's method: step forward with the
            drift, then add a random increment scaled by the square root of the
            step. The square root matters. Brownian increments scale with √dt, not dt,
            which is the numerical shadow of the same fact that makes Itô's
            formula need a second-order term.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's also only order 1/2 in the strong sense, so halving the step
            doesn't halve the error the way it would for a deterministic solver.
            The naive scheme also doesn't respect a process's domain. A CIR path can be
            pushed negative by a large increment even though the true process
            can't go there, which is why the simulation above clamps it.
            The gap between the equation and what a solver does to it is part of
            what the paper is about.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Why volatility scaling with the state changes the problem</summary>
          <p>
            In geometric Brownian motion the diffusion term carries the state, so
            a path near zero moves in small absolute steps and a path high up
            moves in large ones. That's what keeps it positive and makes the
            log-transform work. Take logs and Itô's formula turns a multiplicative
            process into an additive one with constant coefficients.
          </p>
          <p>
            That trick is available because the state appears linearly. The
            nonlinear model is constructed so no such substitution exists, which
            is exactly the property being tested.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <dl class="meta-grid paper-meta">
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd>
            Wiener processes · Itô integrals · Itô's formula · Stochastic
            differential equations · Analytical solvability · Probabilistic
            modeling
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Models</dt>
          <dd>
            Geometric Brownian motion · Ornstein–Uhlenbeck ·
            Cox–Ingersoll–Ross · a stochastic logistic model
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Status</dt>
          <dd>
            Complete and awaiting submission to the IB. Not peer reviewed and
            not affiliated with any institution.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Availability</dt>
          <dd>
            Not published while it is still awaiting submission. Available on
            request afterwards.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="ia">
    <article class="paper">
      <header class="paper-head">
        <div>
          <p class="mono paper-kicker">
            IB Internal Assessment · Physics · complete
          </p>
          <h2 class="h-md" id="ia">
            Temperature and the discharge time constant of an electrolytic
            capacitor
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="status is-done mono">Submitted and graded</p>
      </header>

      <div class="narrow paper-body">
        <p class="rq">
          How does the temperature of an electrolytic capacitor affect its
          effective discharge time constant, τ, in an RC circuit?
        </p>

        <p>
          This one came directly out of robotics. Part of what I did at
          competition was manage the battery and electrical system, and
          components that have been running for a while are warm. I wanted to
          know whether that actually changes their behavior in a way you could
          measure.
        </p>

        <p>
          An ideal capacitor discharging through a resistor decays exponentially,
        </p>

        <Math display n="3" label="V of t equals V nought times e to the minus t over tau, with tau equal to R C">
          V(t) = V<sub>0</sub><span class="op">e</span><sup>−t/τ</sup>,
          &nbsp;&nbsp; τ = RC
        </Math>

        <p>
          Real electrolytic capacitors leak. Imperfections in the aluminium oxide
          dielectric let a small current through, and that leakage rises with
          temperature as charge carriers pick up thermal energy. The leakage acts
          like a resistance in parallel with the capacitor, which lowers the
          effective resistance and shortens{" "}
          <Math label="tau">τ</Math>.
        </p>

        <p>
          I measured discharge curves at five temperatures from 10 °C to 50 °C
          through a fixed 47 kΩ resistor. Taking logs linearizes the decay,
        </p>

        <Math display n="4" label="natural log of V equals natural log of V nought minus t over tau">
          <span class="op">ln</span> V(t) =
          <span class="op">ln</span> V<sub>0</sub> −
          <span class="frac"><span>t</span><span>τ</span></span>
        </Math>

        <p>
          so a plot of{" "}
          <Math label="natural log V against t"><span class="op">ln</span> V</Math>{" "}
          against <Math label="t">t</Math> is a straight line of gradient{" "}
          <Math label="m equals minus one over tau">m = −1/τ</Math>, and{" "}
          <Math label="tau equals minus one over m">τ = −1/m</Math> falls
          straight out of the regression. Linearizing rather than curve-fitting
          was deliberate: departures from exponential behavior show up as visible
          curvature instead of hiding inside a fit.
        </p>

        <div class="ia-results">
          <div class="ia-stat">
            <p class="mono ia-label">τ at 10 °C</p>
            <p class="ia-val">46.7 ± 0.4 s</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ia-stat">
            <p class="mono ia-label">τ at 50 °C</p>
            <p class="ia-val">36.8 ± 0.4 s</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ia-stat">
            <p class="mono ia-label">Change</p>
            <p class="ia-val">−21% over 40 °C</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ia-stat">
            <p class="mono ia-label">Gradient</p>
            <p class="ia-val">−0.247 s/°C</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          τ fell monotonically across the range, and the step between adjacent
          temperatures was about six times the measurement uncertainty, so the effect
          is real rather than noise. Every linearized fit had R² above 0.9995,
          which also confirms the discharge stayed single-exponential throughout.
        </p>

        <p>
          The part I find more interesting is where it disagreed with me. I
          predicted an Arrhenius-type response: since dielectric conductivity
          depends exponentially on temperature, each successive 10 °C step should
          have produced a progressively larger drop in{" "}
          <Math label="tau">τ</Math>. It didn't. The steps came out near-uniform,
          and a straight line fits the whole 40 °C range,
        </p>

        <Math
          display
          n="5"
          label="tau of T equals minus zero point two four seven plus or minus zero point zero nine seven, times T, plus forty nine point zero seven seconds, R squared equals zero point nine nine nine three"
        >
          τ(T) = (−0.247 ± 0.097)T + 49.07 s,
          &nbsp;&nbsp; R<sup>2</sup> = 0.9993
        </Math>

        <p>
          Over this span the response is linear, and the exponential curvature
          the mechanism predicts doesn't show up in the data at all.
        </p>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Uncertainty, and why I didn't just use a trendline</summary>
          <p>
            Rather than take a single regression gradient at face value, I wrote
            the analysis in Python and searched for the extreme gradients still
            consistent with every point's error bar: the steepest and shallowest lines
            that stay feasible given ±0.01 V on each reading. That
            bounds the gradient honestly instead of reporting a fit statistic as
            though it were an uncertainty.
          </p>
          <p>
            The uncertainty in <Math label="tau">τ</Math> then follows from the
            reciprocal relationship,
          </p>

          <Math display label="delta tau equals tau squared times delta m">
            Δτ = τ<sup>2</sup> Δm
          </Math>

          <p>
            which is what puts the ±0.4 s on each value above.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <dl class="meta-grid paper-meta">
        <div>
          <dt>Method</dt>
          <dd>
            RC discharge at 10–50 °C · fixed 47 kΩ resistor · log-linearization ·
            gradient-bounded uncertainty analysis in Python
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Result</dt>
          <dd>
            τ decreases linearly with temperature over this range, contradicting
            the predicted Arrhenius-type curvature.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Status</dt>
          <dd>Submitted and graded, summer 2026. Available on request.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="math">
    <div class="rubric">
      <p class="mono" id="math">Mathematics</p>
    </div>

    <div class="narrow">
      <h2 class="h-md">{smt.name}</h2>
      <p class="dim smt-line">Participant.</p>
      <p>
        Competition math is a different skill from the research above. It rewards
        speed and pattern recognition under a clock rather than working slowly
        through a construction. Both are useful, and I'd rather be doing the
        second one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="where">
    <div class="rubric">
      <p class="mono" id="where">Where this connects</p>
    </div>

    <div class="narrow">
      <p>
        Neither of these is separate from the engineering. The physics
        investigation came straight out of managing the electrical system on{" "}
        <a href="/work/frc5417/">the robot</a>. I wanted to know whether warm
        components behave differently in a way I could actually measure. They do,
        by 21%.
      </p>

      <p>
        The extended essay connects less directly but more deeply. Ratings in{" "}
        <a href="/work/frcmob/">FRCMOB</a> are an estimation problem under
        uncertainty: you're inferring a team's strength from a small, noisy,
        unevenly distributed sample, and the honest version of that answer
        carries a confidence with it. Asking how far exact analysis will take you
        before you have to simulate is the same question as asking how much a
        rating built on four matches is really telling you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Both also taught me the same thing about evidence. The physics data
        contradicted my hypothesis, and the right move was to report that rather
        than round it toward the prediction. That's the same discipline behind
        holding out an event to benchmark a detector.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* One measure for the whole article. Prose, equations, tables, and figures
     all share it, so nothing sits in its own column. The reading measure is
     kept on the paragraphs themselves rather than on the container. */
  .paper {
    max-width: 62rem;
  }

  .paper .narrow,
  .paper .paper-body > p,
  .paper .figure-intro {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* Display equations centre within the prose column, not the wider article,
     so they read as part of the sentence they belong to. */
  .paper .paper-body > .math-display {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .page-head {
    padding-top: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }

  .page-head .mono {
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }

  .page-head h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .paper-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .paper-kicker {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .status {
    border: 1px solid var(--flag);
    color: var(--flag);
    padding: 0.28rem 0.6rem;
    border-radius: 2px;
    font-size: 0.64rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .status.is-done {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The formal research question, set apart from the prose. */
  .rq {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .model-table {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin: 2rem 0 2.25rem;
  }

  .model-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 13rem) minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem 1.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 1.15rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .model-tag {
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .model-h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    font-weight: 520;
  }

  .model-note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.87rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .model-row .math {
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .model-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.7rem;
    }
  }

  .ia-results {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    margin: 1.9rem 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .ia-stat {
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 0.9rem 0.95rem 1rem;
  }

  .ia-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
  }

  .ia-val {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 560;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .figure-block {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .figure-intro {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .paper-meta {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    max-width: 60rem;
  }

  .smt-line {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
  }
</style>
